  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$(3-x)^{4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3-x)^{4},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=-x,$ $n=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3-x)^{4}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3-x)}^{4} & =(\\frac{4}{0}){(3)}^{4}{(-x)}^{0}+(\\frac{4}{1}){(3)}^{4-1}{(-x)}^{1}+(\\frac{4}{2}){(3)}^{4-2}{(-x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{4}+4{(3)}^{3}{(-x)}^{1}+6{(3)}^{2}{(-x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =81-108x+54{x}^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
